WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.935
Maybe later you're 
going to be at a protest,

00:00:01.935 --> 00:00:03.820
and someone's going to 
have a poster that says,

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:06.749
"They buried us, but they 
didn't know we&nbsp;were seeds."

00:00:06.749 --> 00:00:08.201
That's poetry.

00:00:08.201 --> 00:00:10.365
You might be in your 
U.S. History class,

00:00:10.365 --> 00:00:12.284
and your teacher 
may&nbsp;play a video of

00:00:12.284 --> 00:00:14.502
Martin Luther King Jr. 
saying: "We will be able to

00:00:14.502 --> 00:00:18.177
hew out of this mountain&nbsp;
of despair a stone of hope."

00:00:18.177 --> 00:00:19.512
That's poetry.

00:00:19.512 --> 00:00:21.737
Or maybe even here, 
in New York City,

00:00:21.737 --> 00:00:23.773
you're going to go visit 
the Statue of Liberty

00:00:23.773 --> 00:00:26.489
where there's a sonnet that 
declares,&nbsp;as Americans,

00:00:26.489 --> 00:00:29.138
"Give us your tired, 
your poor,

00:00:29.138 --> 00:00:31.717
your huddled masses 
yearning to be free."

00:00:31.717 --> 00:00:34.000
So you see, when 
someone asks me

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.607
to write a poem 
that's not political,

00:00:36.607 --> 00:00:38.620
what they're&nbsp;really 
asking me is to

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.326
not ask charged and 
challenging questions

00:00:41.326 --> 00:00:42.675
in my poetic work,

00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:43.975
and&nbsp;that does not work,

00:00:43.975 --> 00:00:46.708
because poetry is 
always at the pulse

00:00:46.708 --> 00:00:48.917
of the most dangerous 
and the most daring

00:00:48.917 --> 00:00:52.093
questions that a nation 
or a world might face.

